          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Release and Negotiations</strong><br>\nIn 1990, Mandela was released from prison amid growing domestic and international pressure. He immediately engaged in negotiations to end apartheid, leading to the 1994 multi-racial general election. Mandela's release was a pivotal moment in South African history and marked the beginning of a transition to democracy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Presidency and Reconciliation</strong><br>\nMandela was elected South Africa's first black president in 1994. He focused on reconciliation between the country's racial groups and established the Truth and Reconciliation Commission to investigate past human rights abuses. His presidency was dedicated to uniting the nation and building a new future for all South Africans.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nNelson Mandela's legacy is characterized by his commitment to peace, equality, and human rights. He retired from active politics in 1999 but continued to be involved in social issues until his health declined. Mandela passed away on December 5, 2013, but he remains a global icon for freedom and the power of forgiveness.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
